#1 Block syntax

#2 Headings and paragraphs

Use `#1` through `#6` for heading levels. Consecutive text becomes a paragraph; blank lines separate blocks.

#2 Lists and quotes

```text
- unordered list
  - nested item
1. numbered list
- [o] task item
> quoted text
---
```

#2 Code, math, and comments

Fenced code blocks preserve source text; `$$` introduces a math block and `%%` marks a comment block that is not rendered.

#2 Directive blocks

```text
&note[title="A note"]
This block keeps its semantic identity.
&end
```

Directives can nest and their attributes are checked by the registry. Unclosed or unknown structures produce diagnostics instead of disappearing silently.

#3 Compact syntax

Canonical names and short aliases are equivalent:

```sm
&note [title="A note"]
Canonical name.
&end

&n [title="A note"]
Compact name; `&e` is equivalent to `&end`.
&e
```

The official aliases are `&n` (`&note`), `&t` (`&test`), and `&w` (`&warning`). Attributes may touch the name or be separated by whitespace; flag attributes such as `[typing]` have the value `true`.
